#{[17592186329802 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{x\\to \\pi }\\operatorname{cot}⁡x.$"] [17592186329854 :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is defined at $x=1$” is false. From the graph we can see that no value has been assigned to $f(x)$ at $x=1.$\n\n- Statement “$\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)$ exists” is true. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x) & =1, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$” is false. The function is not even defined at $x=1.$ The graph contains a break at $x=1.$ It's impossible to draw the graph of $f(x)$ without taking our pencil off of the paper."] [17592186329857 :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement “$\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)$ is finite” is true. We get $\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=1.$\n\n- Statement “$\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)$ is **not** finite” is false. We get $\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)=1.$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$  is  continuous at $x=-1$” is true. The graph of the function doesn't have a break at $x=-1$ and we can draw the graph of $f(x)$ around $x=-1$ without taking our pencil off of the paper."] [17592186329864 :question/worked-solution "The function $p(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $p(2)=2$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 2}p(x)=1$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 2}p(x)\\ne p(2)$ $\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=2$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\n![](images/fb/fbc65d4168ce2f6747e75a606b3ccc175f9a68d98f718e473255f085d929c334.png)\n\n  \n\nNotice that the functions $h(x)$ and $q(x)$ are continuous at $x=2,$ while $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ both have a discontinuity at $x=2$ but they are *not* removable."] [17592186329868 :question/worked-solution "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=1.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(1)=1$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to f(x)}$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=2,\\,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=1$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nNote that there are also discontinuities at $x=-5$ and $x=-1,$ but they are not jump discontinuities.\n\nHence, the correct answer is $x=1$ only."] [17592186329870 :question/worked-solution "The function has a discontinuity at $x=-2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(-2)=0$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)=1$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)\\ne f(-2)\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=-2$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nNote that the function also has a discontinuity at $x=2.$ but it is not a point discontinuity. Checking the conditions, we find that\n\n- $f(2)=0$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\times $\n\nSo this time, the second condition failed, and therefore $x=2$ is not a point discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, the correct answer is $x=-2$ only."] [17592186329872 :question/worked-solution "The function has a discontinuity at $x=3.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(3)=1$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 3}f(x)=0$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 3}f(x)\\ne f(3)\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=3$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nNote that the function also has a discontinuity at $x=-3,$ but it is not a point discontinuity. Checking the conditions, we find that\n\n- $f(-3)$ does not exist. $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo this time, the first condition failed, and therefore $x=-3$ is not a point discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, the correct answer is $x=3$ only."] [17592186329873 :question/worked-solution "For $x=1,$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $f(1)=1-{1}^{3}=0$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}({x}^{2}+3)=({1}^{2}+3)=4\n$$\n\nwhile \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(1-{x}^{3})=(1-{1}^{3})=0.\n$$\n\nSo both the left and right limits exist and are finite, but they are not equal. $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=1.$\n\nHence, “It has a jump discontinuity at $x=1.$” is true, while “It has a removable discontinuity at $x=1.$” and “It's continuous at $x=1.$” are false."] [17592186329874 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10x)}{2{x}^{3}}-1)=\n$$"] [17592186329874 :question/worked-solution "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since $2{x}^{3}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10x)$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10x)}{2{x}^{3}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10x)}{2{x}^{3}}-1)=0-1=-1.\n$$"] [17592186329875 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{2}{e}^{-x}-2).$"] [17592186329876 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x){e}^{-x}+5).$"] [17592186329877 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}.$"] [17592186329877 :question/worked-solution "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since $x≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}=0.\n$$"] [17592186329879 :question/worked-solution "To determine whether a function $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=2,$ we need to check whether the right-sided limit is equal to the function value.\n\nThe function plotted below has a discontinuity at $x=2,$ but is right-continuous at $x=2.$\n\n![](images/b0/b0c5521372b10882b6f5357cb678aef1274df935e7626bec930e3e084674ba62.png)\n\nIndeed, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)=1=f(2).\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=2.$"] [17592186329912 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{|7x-4|}{2x-6}=\n$$"] [17592186329929 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{|6x-2|}{8x+5}=\n$$"] [17592186329932 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}+1}.$"] [17592186329933 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{|10x+3|}{6-5x}.$"] [17592186329940 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{|6x-4|}{3x+4}=\n$$"] [17592186330053 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[5{(4+\\frac{i}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]"] [17592186330142 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[5{(3+\\frac{i}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]"] [17592186330319 :answer/value "\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{-}}({\\int }_{a}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}x)"] [17592186330402 :answer/value "\\lim_{a\\to (-1)^{+}}({\\int }_{a}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}x)"] [17592186330426 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}[{(2+\\frac{2m}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]"] [17592186330438 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(4+\\frac{2i}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]"] [17592186330650 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(2+\\frac{2i}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]"] [17592186330761 :answer/value "\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }({\\int }_{a}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}x)"] [17592186331020 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}[{(3+\\frac{m}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]"] [17592186331027 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(4+\\frac{i}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]"] [17592186331077 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[5{(4+\\frac{i}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]"] [17592186331171 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[3\\sqrt{\\frac{j}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]"] [17592186331221 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[3\\sqrt{2+\\frac{4j}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]"] [17592186331245 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[5{(1+\\frac{3i}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]"] [17592186331344 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[3\\sqrt{\\frac{4j}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]"] [17592186331382 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[3\\sqrt{2+\\frac{2j}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]"] [17592186331515 :answer/value "\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}({\\int }_{a}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}x)"] [17592186331723 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[5{(3+\\frac{3i}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]"] [17592186331729 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(2+\\frac{4i}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]"] [17592186331884 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}[{(3+\\frac{2m}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]"] [17592186331903 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(2+\\frac{4i}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]"] [17592186331940 :answer/value "\\lim_{a\\to +\\infty }({\\int }_{-1}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}x)"] [17592186331975 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}[{(2+\\frac{m}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]"] [17592186332019 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}[{(4+\\frac{m}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]"] [17592186332117 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[3\\sqrt{2+\\frac{3j}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]"] [17592186332681 :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{e}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+\\frac{ie}{n})\\cdot \\frac{e}{n}].\n$$"] [17592186332681 :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=e.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{e}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{e}{n}=\\frac{b-e}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=2e.\n$$\n\nConsequently,  ${x}_{i}=e+\\frac{ie}{n}$ and $f({x}_{i})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{e}^{2e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+\\frac{ie}{n})\\cdot \\frac{e}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and $b=2e.$"] [17592186332769 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches at infinity,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡n}{n+2}.\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡n}{n+2}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence converges,  and its limit is $0.$"] [17592186332770 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nTo find the limit of each sequence, we must evaluate them for $n\\to \\infty .$\n\nFor ${a}_{n}=\\frac{n\\sqrt{n}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n)},$ both the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nFor ${b}_{n}=\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡(2n)}{{e}^{n}},$ both the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{b}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nFor ${c}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{5}}{{e}^{n}},$ both the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{c}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"${a}_{n}$ only.\""] [17592186332842 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-1}{x}.$"] [17592186332843 :question/worked-solution "- Statement “It is differentiable at $x=-2$” is false. Continuity does not imply differentiability. For example, the function $y=|x+2|$ is continuous but it's not differentiable at $x=-2.$\n\n- Statement “It's **not** differentiable at $x=1$” is true. The function has a cusp at $x=1$, so it's not differentiable there.\n\n- Statement $\\lim_{x\\to 3}f(x)=f(3).$ is true. Differentiability implies continuity. Hence, $\\lim_{x\\to 3}f(x)=f(3).$"] [17592186333252 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x)=0"] [17592186333277 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x)=2"] [17592186333723 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x)=1"] [17592186333835 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x)=-2"] [17592186335230 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x)=\\text{DNE}"] [17592186335802 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186335934 :question/problem "Given that $p(x)=4{x}^{3}$ and $q(x)=3{x}^{3}$, what is $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)}?$"] [17592186335942 :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}f(x),$ we must compute the left and right-sided limits separately.\n\nHowever, the left-sided limit $\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)$ does not exist since $f(x)$ is not defined on the interval $x\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nConsequently, the overall limit does not exist either:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"] [17592186335943 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{3{x}^{4}+2}{{x}^{3}-7x}.$"] [17592186335944 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{3}+x}{2{x}^{2}+x+4}.$"] [17592186335945 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{4{x}^{5}-9}{{x}^{3}+8x}.$"] [17592186335949 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{5}}.$"] [17592186335949 :question/worked-solution "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{x}≫{x}^{5}$ for large values of $x,$ the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{5}}=\\infty .\n$$"] [17592186335951 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x){e}^{-2x}+3).$"] [17592186335957 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\left(-\\frac{2x}{9\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\right).$"] [17592186335958 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x}{3x}.$"] [17592186336251 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)\\ne f(2)"] [17592186336352 :answer/value "$\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)$ does not exist"] [17592186336453 :answer/value "$\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist"] [17592186337129 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)\\ne f(-1)"] [17592186337223 :answer/value "$\\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x)$ does not exist"] [17592186337413 :answer/value "$\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)$ does not exist"] [17592186337570 :answer/value "$\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)$ does not exist"] [17592186337581 :answer/value "$\\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x)$ does not exist"] [17592186337841 :answer/value "$\\lim_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)$ does not exist"] [17592186337865 :answer/value "$\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)$ does not exist"] [17592186338059 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)\\ne f(-2)"] [17592186338236 :answer/value "$\\lim_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)$ does not exist"] [17592186338887 :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1$ and $\\lim_{x\\to {(-1)}^{-}}f(x)=8,$ then what is the value of $\\sqrt{f(-1)+1}?$"] [17592186338888 :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$ and $f(0)=2,$ then what is the value of\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}{({(f(x))}^{2}-3)}^{2}\\,?\n$$"] [17592186338889 :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=4$ and $f(4)=2,$ then what is $\\lim_{x\\to 4}{({x}^{2}-6f(x))}^{2}?$"] [17592186339089 :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{100x}{{x}^{2}-1}=$"] [17592186339090 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{1/x}}.$"] [17592186339211 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}[4(2+\\frac{5m}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]"] [17592186339511 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[-5{\\left(\\frac{4j}{n}\\right)}^{-4}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]"] [17592186339932 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}[(1+\\frac{20m}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]"] [17592186340041 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[-5{(-2+\\frac{4j}{n})}^{-2}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]"] [17592186340692 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[-5{(4+\\frac{2j}{n})}^{-2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]"] [17592186340705 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}[ln⁡(1+\\frac{5m}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]"] [17592186341133 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}[ln⁡(3+\\frac{20m}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]"] [17592186341232 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[-5{(-4+\\frac{2j}{n})}^{-2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]"] [17592186341237 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[-5{\\left(\\frac{j}{n}\\right)}^{-2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]"] [17592186341340 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}[ln⁡(1+\\frac{20m}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]"] [17592186341900 :question/worked-solution "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=-1.$ Checking our conditions, we have\n\n- $f(-1)=4$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -1}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=4,\\,\\,\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)=2.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=-1$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nNote that there are also discontinuities at $x=-3$ and $x=3,$ but they are not jump discontinuities.\n\nHence, the correct answer is $x=-1$ only."] [17592186341902 :question/worked-solution "To determine whether $f(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=c,$ we need to check whether the left-sided limit is equal to the function value.\n\nLet's check this for $x=-1$, at $x=1$ and at  $x=3.$\n\n- At $x=-1$ the function is not left-continuous because\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}f(x)=1\\ne -1=f(-1).\n$$\n\n- At $x=1$ the function is not left-continuous because \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=-1\\ne 1=f(1).\n$$\n\n- At $x=3$ the function is left-continuous because\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{3}^{-}}f(x)=1=f(3).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=3$ only."] [17592186341907 :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=-1$ and $f(-1)=-2,$ then what is \n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}\\,\\frac{1}{3f(x)-10x}?\n$$"] [17592186341908 :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “The function is left-continuous at $x=3$” is false. The function is not left-continuous at $x=3,$ since the left-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)=3\\ne 1=f(3)\n$$\n\n- Statement “The function is right-continuous at $x=3$” is true. The function is right-continuous at $x=3,$ since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x)=1=f(3)\n$$\n\n- Statement “The function is continuous at $x=3$” is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=3,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is not left-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement “The function is right-continuous at $x=3$” is true."] [17592186341909 :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “The function is left-continuous at $x=1$” is false. The function is not left-continuous at $x=1,$ since the left-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=1\\ne 3=f(1)\n$$\n\n- Statement “The function is right-continuous at $x=1$” is false. The function is not right-continuous at $x=1,$ since the right-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=1\\ne 3=f(1)\n$$\n\n- Statement “The function is continuous at $x=1$” is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=1,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is neither left-continuous nor right-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, none of the statements are true."] [17592186341931 :question/worked-solution "- Statement $\\lim_{x\\to 4}f(x)=f(4).$ is true. Differentiability implies continuity. Hence, $\\lim_{x\\to 4}f(x)=f(4).$\n\n- Statement “It is **not** differentiable at $x=2$” is true. The function has a corner point at $x=2$, so it's not differentiable there.\n\n- Statement “It is differentiable at $x=0$” is false.  Continuity does not imply differentiability. For instance, the function  $g(x)=|x|$ is continuous at $x=0$ but it is not differentiable there."]}
